1
00:00:01,540 --> 00:00:04,082
To continue our discussion
of kernel ioctl, and

2
00:00:04,082 --> 00:00:07,244
talking about to understand
kernel ioctl and its purpose and

3
00:00:07,244 --> 00:00:09,851
how to write an ioctl handler for
a kernel driver.

4
00:00:11,140 --> 00:00:15,411
So ioctl is a system call on a file
descriptor this passed to driver.

5
00:00:15,411 --> 00:00:18,283
So it's similar to the reader
right system calls, but

6
00:00:18,283 --> 00:00:21,530
a little bit more unstructured in
terms of how it can be used and

7
00:00:21,530 --> 00:00:24,300
a little bit more flexible
in terms of how you use it.

8
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:28,180
So with the ioctl, you're identifying
a specific command to be performed and

9
00:00:28,180 --> 00:00:30,844
then you're providing some
argument to the driver.

10
00:00:30,844 --> 00:00:34,027
And oftentimes,
that argument is a pointer and oftentimes,

11
00:00:34,027 --> 00:00:38,128
that that pointer is referencing memory
that you want to either transfer into or

12
00:00:38,128 --> 00:00:40,961
out of your driver to perform
some specific function.

13
00:00:42,340 --> 00:00:46,529
So this differs from read or write where
you're actually passing a buffer of data

14
00:00:46,529 --> 00:00:49,240
into or out of the device
in a structured direction,

15
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:52,140
based on the implementation
of the function.

16
00:00:52,140 --> 00:00:53,540
So one difference.

17
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:58,525
Another difference from my ioctl is
that you can't access it from user

18
00:00:58,525 --> 00:01:02,748
space as we've been doing with
like cat and echo commands,

19
00:01:02,748 --> 00:01:07,733
you need another program or maybe like
a Python script that's going to be

20
00:01:07,733 --> 00:01:12,640
able to issue the ioctl
command through a system call.

21
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:15,612
So it's really designed as a common
interface that can be used for

22
00:01:15,612 --> 00:01:16,440
device control.

23
00:01:16,440 --> 00:01:21,422
So one of the ways that you
think about how ioctl is used

24
00:01:21,422 --> 00:01:26,204
in practice today would be like a CD or
DVD RAM tray.

25
00:01:26,204 --> 00:01:28,890
If you wanted to eject the media
from the CD or DVD RAM,

26
00:01:28,890 --> 00:01:32,090
that's kind of like a classic
example of what an ioctl would do.

27
00:01:32,090 --> 00:01:35,646
So it's something that maybe doesn't quite

28
00:01:35,646 --> 00:01:40,340
fit in the mold of something
related to reading or writing.

29
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:45,254
So a read or write operation to a CD RAM
or DVD RAM would be writing data onto

30
00:01:45,254 --> 00:01:49,710
the media, but ioctl is an out of
band type of control interface.

31
00:01:49,710 --> 00:01:52,590
So ejecting media is kind of
like a classic example.

32
00:01:52,590 --> 00:01:56,928
You could also think if your device had
some door that had a physical lock on it,

33
00:01:56,928 --> 00:01:57,980
you could enable or

34
00:01:57,980 --> 00:02:02,160
disable lock through an ioctl that
would probably be an appropriate case.

35
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:06,402
You can also use it to report information
about the device in that buffer that you

36
00:02:06,402 --> 00:02:08,009
pass as one of the arguments.

37
00:02:08,009 --> 00:02:14,331
So you can see the user space prototype
here and it's really the file descriptor,

38
00:02:14,331 --> 00:02:18,189
so that's going to reference
the device endpoint.

39
00:02:18,189 --> 00:02:21,991
The request which is
going to be the command and

40
00:02:21,991 --> 00:02:26,221
then this dot dot dot and
so what do the dots mean?

41
00:02:26,221 --> 00:02:29,331
You're probably familiar with this
already from looking at porn f and

42
00:02:29,331 --> 00:02:30,551
other types of functions.

43
00:02:30,551 --> 00:02:32,759
This just represents.

44
00:02:32,759 --> 00:02:37,413
So it's saying that there could be any
number from a C compilers perspective,

45
00:02:37,413 --> 00:02:41,305
the compiler is going to allow any
number of arguments after ioctl.

46
00:02:41,305 --> 00:02:44,570
But in this case,
it's kind of a historical baggage thing.

47
00:02:44,570 --> 00:02:49,775
There's really always only one
signal argument here, rp but

48
00:02:49,775 --> 00:02:54,900
it was implemented before you
could have a void pointer as C.

49
00:02:54,900 --> 00:02:58,627
And so using the dot dot dot
gets around the fact that

50
00:02:58,627 --> 00:03:01,070
the last argument was not typed.

51
00:03:01,070 --> 00:03:05,409
So again, you can kind of see already we
have a little bit of historical baggage

52
00:03:05,409 --> 00:03:06,561
with this command.

53
00:03:08,040 --> 00:03:12,143
So one of the reasons that we'll talk
about it and the reason is covered in

54
00:03:12,143 --> 00:03:16,855
the book is that it's the easiest and kind
of the most straightforward for these out

55
00:03:16,855 --> 00:03:21,260
of band not really read, not really
write type of driver device operations.

56
00:03:21,260 --> 00:03:24,968
But one of the things that book
mentions is that it's sort of

57
00:03:24,968 --> 00:03:29,197
fallen out of favor as among kernel
developers just because again,

58
00:03:29,197 --> 00:03:32,778
a little bit of that historical
baggage that it carries.

59
00:03:32,778 --> 00:03:37,020
It's difficult to audit, it's often
not well documented how it works.

60
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:41,558
So there are some alternatives that
you should know about if you're

61
00:03:41,558 --> 00:03:43,477
designing your own driver.

62
00:03:43,477 --> 00:03:47,625
For instance, the book talks about you
can embed commands directly into the data

63
00:03:47,625 --> 00:03:50,140
stream and
we'll give an example of that later.

64
00:03:50,140 --> 00:03:53,039
And you can also use virtual
file systems like Sysfs,

65
00:03:53,039 --> 00:03:55,561
which we're going to talk
about in later weeks.

66
00:03:57,340 --> 00:04:01,713
So if you look through the book,
you'll see the ioctl file operations table

67
00:04:01,713 --> 00:04:06,633
signature that it references and remember,
the file operations table is where we map

68
00:04:06,633 --> 00:04:10,491
functions in our driver to functions
the kernel knows how to call.

69
00:04:10,491 --> 00:04:13,903
So that's like read and
write that we've already discussed.

70
00:04:13,903 --> 00:04:18,460
And if you look at the signature that they
reference in the book, it doesn't quite

71
00:04:18,460 --> 00:04:22,440
match what the latest kernel is using,
however, it's very similar.

72
00:04:22,440 --> 00:04:26,126
And I wanted to point this out because
this is a common thing that you'll

73
00:04:26,126 --> 00:04:27,550
see as the kernel changes.

74
00:04:27,550 --> 00:04:32,418
That function prototypes changed slightly
and usually, you can pretty easily

75
00:04:32,418 --> 00:04:37,230
map what the old function prototype was
to what the new function prototype is.

76
00:04:37,230 --> 00:04:40,182
So if you look at the example
that was in the book,

77
00:04:40,182 --> 00:04:45,006
there was this extra inode pointer here,
and if you look at the latest version of

78
00:04:45,006 --> 00:04:49,920
the kernel which you can find link here
on this slide, the inode pointer is gone.

79
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:53,640
And so if you had a driver that was
written with the old implementation,

80
00:04:53,640 --> 00:04:57,794
it was referencing that inode, you get
a compile error when you try to compile it

81
00:04:57,794 --> 00:04:59,550
and you need to modify your code.

82
00:04:59,550 --> 00:05:03,665
But if you look into more detail
about where you can find the inode,

83
00:05:03,665 --> 00:05:08,663
it turns out it's stored as a part of the
file structure that's passed here and so

84
00:05:08,663 --> 00:05:13,441
you could pretty easily just set up your
own inode pointer that just takes file

85
00:05:13,441 --> 00:05:17,520
pointer, file inode and
saves that off as the local inode value.

86
00:05:17,520 --> 00:05:22,180
So reworking your ioctl to use
the new to find kernel mechanism

87
00:05:22,180 --> 00:05:26,680
would be pretty straightforward
most likely in this case.

88
00:05:26,680 --> 00:05:29,793
And the other thing that you'll
notice about the difference in

89
00:05:29,793 --> 00:05:32,964
file operations is that rather
than just being called by ioctl,

90
00:05:32,964 --> 00:05:36,561
there are two different unlocked ioctl and
compact ioctl definitions.

91
00:05:38,640 --> 00:05:43,592
So going back to why we need
new definitions for ioctl,

92
00:05:43,592 --> 00:05:48,231
the book mentions that
the optional arg argument.

93
00:05:48,231 --> 00:05:52,302
So the third argument that we mentioned
that's the dot dot dot from user space,

94
00:05:52,302 --> 00:05:56,252
it's passed in the form of unsigned long
regardless of whether it was given by

95
00:05:56,252 --> 00:05:58,140
the user as an integer to pointer.

96
00:05:58,140 --> 00:05:59,411
So what would be a problem with this?

97
00:05:59,411 --> 00:06:03,754
Well, it means that we're assuming
that size of unsigned long is equal to

98
00:06:03,754 --> 00:06:07,761
size of void, and that might not
be true based on the architecture.

99
00:06:07,761 --> 00:06:08,289
So again,

100
00:06:08,289 --> 00:06:12,070
kind of like a historical baggage thing
that we're dealing here with ioctl.

101
00:06:12,070 --> 00:06:17,608
So the new implementation includes what's
called compact_ioctl that'll handle

102
00:06:17,608 --> 00:06:22,912
the differences in argument size between
64 and 32 bit, and making sure that

103
00:06:22,912 --> 00:06:28,060
old programs running on 32 bit programs
that run on 64 bit Kernels are going to

104
00:06:28,060 --> 00:06:32,760
do the right thing in terms of passing
pointers in that third argument.

105
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:36,150
So that's the whole purpose
of the new compact_ioctl.

106
00:06:36,150 --> 00:06:41,011
If you look at how an ioctl is actually
implemented in a device driver.

107
00:06:41,011 --> 00:06:45,540
So there's some good examples of this in
the device drivers example source code.

108
00:06:45,540 --> 00:06:51,246
So if we look at skull ioctl, the ioctl
code looks like this as a general

109
00:06:51,246 --> 00:06:57,055
design where there's a switch
statement that switches this command,

110
00:06:57,055 --> 00:07:02,320
that's the argument after the file
reference from user space.

111
00:07:02,320 --> 00:07:06,360
And based on that command,
we do some action on the driver.

112
00:07:06,360 --> 00:07:10,203
And so one of the things you notice is
that these commands are defined in what

113
00:07:10,203 --> 00:07:11,789
looks like sort of a weird way,

114
00:07:11,789 --> 00:07:16,371
where it's using these macros and it's got
parameters that's passing into the macro.

115
00:07:16,371 --> 00:07:21,339
So the commands aren't just as simple
as saying 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 and defining

116
00:07:21,339 --> 00:07:26,740
a set of commands for your driver and
we'll talk about that in a minute here.

117
00:07:26,740 --> 00:07:31,570
So why do we make things more complicated,
why are we not just defining commands at

118
00:07:31,570 --> 00:07:36,000
0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 in order based
on whatever the command support?

119
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:39,975
It's really an attempt by the kernel
to try to separate commands and

120
00:07:39,975 --> 00:07:43,324
kind of a sanity check that
the command you're sending is

121
00:07:43,324 --> 00:07:46,240
an appropriate command
to send to the driver.

122
00:07:46,240 --> 00:07:49,585
So because you could
potentially get user space and

123
00:07:49,585 --> 00:07:54,563
kernel space code confused, and you
could have a situation where user space

124
00:07:54,563 --> 00:08:00,040
tries to send a command to a driver and
it happens to be the wrong driver.

125
00:08:00,040 --> 00:08:02,171
Having different sets
of commands defined for

126
00:08:02,171 --> 00:08:05,721
different drivers helps ensure that
the driver might be able to catch that and

127
00:08:05,721 --> 00:08:09,460
say, I can't support this command that
you're trying to send from user space.

128
00:08:09,460 --> 00:08:12,210
So just provides us a little
bit of separation, prevents

129
00:08:12,210 --> 00:08:16,105
you from accidentally doing something on
a driver that you didn't mean to do just

130
00:08:16,105 --> 00:08:19,860
because you sent an ioctl that was valid
for one driver to a different driver.

131
00:08:19,860 --> 00:08:20,440
And again,

132
00:08:20,440 --> 00:08:23,978
you're kind of seeing the reasons why
ioctl maybe a little bit out of favor

133
00:08:23,978 --> 00:08:27,660
here in terms of having to do these
kinds of workarounds in the first place.

134
00:08:27,660 --> 00:08:33,044
So one of the ways that ioctl does this is
by combining a whole bunch of different

135
00:08:33,044 --> 00:08:38,049
information about what you're trying
to do into the command argument.

136
00:08:38,049 --> 00:08:43,007
And so one of the things that puts in
that argument is a what's called a magic

137
00:08:43,007 --> 00:08:47,259
number, that really just is
a character that helps provide some

138
00:08:47,259 --> 00:08:51,540
separation between different
device driver implementation.

139
00:08:51,540 --> 00:08:56,378
And if you look at the source code, you
can see that the magic number that they

140
00:08:56,378 --> 00:09:00,790
chose for the skull driver is k and
that's just arbitrarily chosen.

141
00:09:00,790 --> 00:09:05,775
If you look at the documentation for
the kernel, it has a bunch of lists

142
00:09:05,775 --> 00:09:10,763
of where magic numbers are referenced
in current device drivers, and

143
00:09:10,763 --> 00:09:15,494
you could look at that list and
try to decide what what is unlikely to

144
00:09:15,494 --> 00:09:19,999
conflict with anything I'm going to
be doing in ioctl my driver.

145
00:09:19,999 --> 00:09:24,975
What's unlikely to as a ioctl command
that would be sent to my driver

146
00:09:24,975 --> 00:09:29,630
in addition to these already
existing kernel device drivers.

147
00:09:29,630 --> 00:09:33,248
And in our case it doesn't really
matter because this isn't really

148
00:09:33,248 --> 00:09:34,470
a production kernel.

149
00:09:34,470 --> 00:09:39,623
But again, if you are developing some
kind of production device driver,

150
00:09:39,623 --> 00:09:43,300
this would have to be
something you'd think about.

151
00:09:43,300 --> 00:09:47,919
And again, a reason that maybe you decide
ioctl isn't the right way to implement for

152
00:09:47,919 --> 00:09:50,340
your particular driver.

153
00:09:50,340 --> 00:09:53,572
So in addition to the magic
number that specified,

154
00:09:53,572 --> 00:09:58,460
we're going to wrap that with a macro that
tells us more about what we're trying

155
00:09:58,460 --> 00:10:00,431
to do with this ioctl command.

156
00:10:02,140 --> 00:10:06,210
So that macro is going to be the IO macro.

157
00:10:06,210 --> 00:10:09,239
And so this is going to tell us
really how we're going to use or

158
00:10:09,239 --> 00:10:12,655
if we're going to use that third
parameter that we talked about,

159
00:10:12,655 --> 00:10:15,450
that's the dot dot dot
parameter from user space.

160
00:10:15,450 --> 00:10:19,628
So it's going to tell us whether that's a
pointer that's pointed to a data transfer

161
00:10:19,628 --> 00:10:23,440
going into the driver, or a pointer to
a data transfer that's coming out of

162
00:10:23,440 --> 00:10:25,941
the driver, or
if there's no parameter at all.

163
00:10:25,941 --> 00:10:30,607
And it's just the command that's going to
tell you what the device driver to do, or

164
00:10:30,607 --> 00:10:33,431
if it's one that has
a bidirectional transfer.

165
00:10:33,431 --> 00:10:36,028
So you start by writing some
data into the driver, and

166
00:10:36,028 --> 00:10:39,340
you get a response that includes
data coming back out of the driver.

167
00:10:39,340 --> 00:10:42,240
So all those we encode
with these IO macros.

168
00:10:42,240 --> 00:10:46,539
And really this macro is just
splitting into eight bit chunks,

169
00:10:46,539 --> 00:10:50,352
splitting unsigned integer
into eight bit chunks, and

170
00:10:50,352 --> 00:10:55,057
shifting mask values into that
integer value which you're probably

171
00:10:55,057 --> 00:11:00,440
already familiar with other
embedded programming paradigms.

172
00:11:00,440 --> 00:11:05,945
And so we can use the read,
write, and IOWR as parameters

173
00:11:05,945 --> 00:11:12,961
that can tell us which direction
the data transfer is going to happen in.

174
00:11:15,040 --> 00:11:19,755
So moving past a simpler example here,
if we start with skull IOC reset,

175
00:11:19,755 --> 00:11:24,580
you can see that this is just using
an IO macro with no third argument here.

176
00:11:24,580 --> 00:11:27,571
And what this is saying is that
it's an ioctl with no parameters.

177
00:11:27,571 --> 00:11:32,211
So that dot dot dot or the arg argument
that the optional argument that

178
00:11:32,211 --> 00:11:36,940
specified from the user space,
it's not actually used in this case.

179
00:11:36,940 --> 00:11:40,585
So moving into a more complicated one,

180
00:11:40,585 --> 00:11:45,665
you could also look at skull
IOCS quantum as a an example

181
00:11:45,665 --> 00:11:50,320
that does a transfer as
a part of an ioctl command.

182
00:11:50,320 --> 00:11:54,789
And so it's using the IOW macro
where that's actually ioctl command

183
00:11:54,789 --> 00:11:58,820
that's going to transfer data
from user space into the kernel.

184
00:11:58,820 --> 00:12:03,321
So the write and read here are defined
from the perspective of user space.

185
00:12:03,321 --> 00:12:06,498
A write is going to be writing data
from user space into the kernel, and

186
00:12:06,498 --> 00:12:08,810
a read is going to be doing
the opposite direction.

187
00:12:08,810 --> 00:12:12,807
And if you look at how this macro is
going to work in terms of defining

188
00:12:12,807 --> 00:12:16,510
the command number,
it's going to use the magic number and

189
00:12:16,510 --> 00:12:21,251
it's going to use a incremental command
number that we're going to make as a part

190
00:12:21,251 --> 00:12:26,700
of defining the unique command [COUGH] and
then specifying what you're transferring.

191
00:12:26,700 --> 00:12:31,480
So in this case, we're doing a write from
user space into the kernel of an integer.

192
00:12:31,480 --> 00:12:34,035
And so the check of that integer for

193
00:12:34,035 --> 00:12:38,531
size is going to encode into
the command the size of the data that

194
00:12:38,531 --> 00:12:43,230
we're transferring in with
the associated transfer buffer.

195
00:12:43,230 --> 00:12:47,374
So you're going to use in this
parameter the whatever structure or

196
00:12:47,374 --> 00:12:51,980
type references, the amount of data
that you're transferring into or

197
00:12:51,980 --> 00:12:54,761
out of the kernel with your ioctl command.

198
00:12:56,740 --> 00:13:00,412
So there are some ioctls that
are recognized by the kernel and

199
00:13:00,412 --> 00:13:03,310
decoded before passing
to your device driver.

200
00:13:03,310 --> 00:13:07,348
And really T is the magic type if
you happen to choose that one,

201
00:13:07,348 --> 00:13:11,466
there's a chance that if you
defined your command just wrong,

202
00:13:11,466 --> 00:13:13,971
you might never get it to your driver.

203
00:13:13,971 --> 00:13:17,926
So this is just something that the book
mentions as something to think about when

204
00:13:17,926 --> 00:13:19,351
picking your magic number.

205
00:13:21,140 --> 00:13:25,985
So we talked before about how ioctl
fallen out of favor a little bit with

206
00:13:25,985 --> 00:13:27,480
kernel developers.

207
00:13:27,480 --> 00:13:31,311
So there are two things that the book
mentions you can use instead of ioctl, and

208
00:13:31,311 --> 00:13:34,522
you should probably consider if
you're starting from scratch.

209
00:13:34,522 --> 00:13:37,101
Ioctls around and
you're definitely going to see it.

210
00:13:37,101 --> 00:13:40,225
So it's worth knowing about it and
understanding it but

211
00:13:40,225 --> 00:13:44,900
there are a couple of alternatives, one of
them will talk about now which is write.

212
00:13:44,900 --> 00:13:49,825
So the book mentions that if you have
a device especially device that doesn't

213
00:13:49,825 --> 00:13:54,900
transfer data in and out normally, you can
actually just not use ioctl at all and

214
00:13:54,900 --> 00:13:57,810
just write control
sequences to the device.

215
00:13:57,810 --> 00:14:02,627
And so you could for us to just say write
start as a string to your device name,

216
00:14:02,627 --> 00:14:07,147
and then your device could be parsing
the data that gets written through

217
00:14:07,147 --> 00:14:11,250
the write command and
deciding what to do in terms of operation.

218
00:14:11,250 --> 00:14:13,431
So if you have a device
that doesn't transfer data,

219
00:14:13,431 --> 00:14:14,981
this might be a way that makes sense.

